<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Caere</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 273 BCE (approx.) · capital Caere</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Coastal Etruscan city, now Cerveteri, famed for its vast tomb-city of the Banditaccia and its ports open to Greek and Phoenician traders.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Caere, Etruscan Caisra, Greek Agylla, rose on a tufa plateau near the Tyrrhenian coast north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Villanovan villages united into a city during the 8th century BCE, and by the 7th century the princely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regolini-galassi-tomb">Regolini-Galassi tomb</a> shows great wealth from metals and long-distance trade.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Caere looked to the sea through its ports, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi">Pyrgi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsium">Alsium</a> and Punicum. At Pyrgi the gold tablets of about 500 BCE, written in Etruscan and Phoenician, record king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thefarie-velianas">Thefarie Velianas</a> dedicating a shrine to the goddess Uni-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>. Caere joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaean Greeks</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a> around 540 BCE, kept a treasury at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a> and produced painted Caeretan hydriae and the famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarcophagus-of-the-spouses">Sarcophagus of the Spouses</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Caere was long on friendly terms with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, sheltering Roman sacred objects during the Gallic sack of 390 BCE and receiving a form of citizenship without the vote. After a brief revolt it lost half its territory in 273 BCE and became a Roman town. Its necropolises are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/caere.jpg" alt="Caere" width="320" height="389"><figcaption>Image: NormanEinstein · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_civilization_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 273 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caere</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thefarie Velianas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Etruscan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Etruscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cerveteri, Caisra, Agylla</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-586&amp;focus=caere" aria-label="Caere on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4974 682 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Caere lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5294 938L5290 918M5294 938L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5290 918h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5294 938h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5294 938h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-586&amp;focus=caere">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

